* Roadmap

- **Why code cosmetics matter with AI**: an agent writes more code than a human
  can read, so machines must check the form of the code
- **Style and naming**: names, spelling, code stutter, the form of comments and
  docstrings, and the skills that fix them
- **Linting**: modifying and non-modifying checks, pre-commit hooks, and the
  linter inside the agent loop and in CI
- **Imports and types**: one import style, full-path imports, import cycle
  detection, type hints, and common `mypy` errors
- **Unit testing**: test conventions, whole-output comparison, golden files,
  speed tiers, and tests written by an agent
- **Code coverage**: measurement, coverage gates per suite, mutation testing,
  and flaky tests
- **Keeping the agent honest**: how an agent games a gate, and the running
  example after all the fixes

# Why Code Cosmetics Matter With AI

* Agents Write More Code Than Humans Can Read
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:43 '\subsection{Motivation}'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:213 '#### The Writer Is the Reader'

- @Problem@: an agent writes a diff in minutes, and a human reviews it in hours
  - Human attention is the scarce resource, not code
  - Manual review cannot catch every violation as the codebase grows

- @Fact@: code is written once and read many times
  - The readers are a reviewer, a teammate, the author in two months, and the
    next agent session

- @Example@: a reviewer opens a large PR written by an agent
  - Three naming styles, two import styles, and no type hints
  - The reviewer spends the time on the form and has none left for the logic

- @Key idea@: automate every check that needs no judgment, so the human reviews
  only what needs a human

* Code Cosmetics: Definition
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:146 '## Meta'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:2357 '### Goal'

- @Definition@: **code cosmetics** are the conventions that a machine can check
  without understanding what the code does
  - _Form_: names, comments, docstrings, imports, type hints, formatting
  - _Evidence_: unit tests and coverage, the minimum proof that every change
    carries

- @Characteristics@: a convention is a decision made once
  - There is no right or wrong, e.g., the "tabs vs spaces" debate
  - A simple rule that applies without checking the docs beats an optimal rule
    with corner cases

- @Example@: the repo style guide forks the **Google Python Style Guide** and
  **PEP 8** _(van Rossum et al., 2001)_, then adds the team decisions

- @Key idea@: once a convention is stable, automate it, so that reviewers look
  for bugs and design flaws instead of lints

* The Enforcement Ladder
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:983 '* Pre-Commit Hooks'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:55 '\subsection{Summary}'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **enforcement ladder** is the list of places that check a
  convention, from the cheapest to the most expensive

- @Example@: the rule _"no `import *`"_
  - Starts as a line in the style guide
  - Goes into the agent rules file
  - Becomes `amp_check_import`, run by the hooks and by CI

- @Key idea@: a convention climbs the ladder as it stabilizes, and each check
  runs at the lowest rung that catches the problem
  - The top two rungs are advice, the next three are code that runs every
    time
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph Ladder {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
