# Imports and Types

* One Import Style
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1056 '#### Don'T Use Evil `import *`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1086 '#### Avoid `from ... import ...`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1119 '#### Exceptions to the Import Style'

- @Problem@: `from ... import *` and `from ... import name` imports
  - Hide where each name comes from, e.g., which `read_data()`?
  - Collide, since many modules define the same name
  - Need an edit of the import line for each new function used

- @Solution@: every import starts with `import` and binds a short alias
  ```python
  # Bad.
  from helpers.hsystem import *
  from helpers import hdbg
  # Good.
  import helpers.hsystem as hsystem
  import helpers.hdbg as hdbg
  ```

- @Remark@: two exceptions
  - `from typing import List, Optional`, so that type hints stay short
  - `import *` in an `__init__.py` to define the public interface of a package

- @Key idea@: the alias at the call site tells the reader, the agent, and
  `grep` where the code lives, e.g., `hsystem.system_to_files()`

* Full-Path Imports and Canonical Aliases
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1131 '#### Always Import with a Full Path From the Root of the Repo / Submodule'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1143 '#### Baptizing Module Import'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:250 '\subsubsection{Rules for Imports}'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **full-path import** starts from the repo root, so the code
  runs the same from any dir
  - `import datapull.ccxt.data.extract.exchange_class`, not
    `import exchange_class`

- @Definition@: a **canonical alias** is the one short name that all files use
  for a module, declared in its docstring
  - Built from the path initials, e.g., `dataflow.core` becomes `dtfcore`
  - At most 8 characters, checked by `amp_check_import`

- @Pros@: code moves between files with no change at the call sites, and an
  agent that sees `hgit.` in one file writes `hgit.` in the next
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```python
"""
Market data access.

Import as:

import market_data
  as mdata
"""
```

::::
:::

* Import Cycle Detection
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:176 '\subsection{Import Cycle Detection}'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/codebase/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md:49 '### Rules for imports'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=62%}
- @Definition@: an **import cycle** is a loop in the graph of module imports
  - It fails at import time or at run time, and couples modules

- @Solution@: rules that keep the graph acyclic
  - Inside a package, import the sibling files
  - Outside a package, import the package
  - Only module-level imports, never one inside a function

- @Techniques@:
  - `detect_import_cycles` fails on a cycle and lists its files, in CI via
    `invoke lint_detect_cycles`
  - `show_imports` draws the dependency graph

- @Remark@: the package hierarchy is covered in detail in Lesson 13.01
::::
:::: {.column width=38%}

```graphviz
digraph Cycle {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Courier", fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // module : blue | forbidden edge : coral

    Hgit    [label="hgit",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Hsystem [label="hsystem",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Hdbg    [label="hdbg",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Hgit -> Hsystem;
    Hsystem -> Hdbg;
    Hdbg -> Hgit [color="#D98C8C", style=dashed, fontcolor="#6B2A2A",
                  label="  Cycle"];
}
```
label=fig:import-cycle
caption=Allowed imports point down a layer (gray), and one upward import (coral) closes a cycle

::::
:::

* Type Hints
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.type_hints.how_to_guide.md:24 '## Why We Use Type Hints'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.type_hints.how_to_guide.md:32 '## What to Annotate with Type Hints'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:15 '## Use `typing` Module Style for Type Hints'

- @Motivation@: type hints _(van Rossum et al., 2014)_ are documentation that
  cannot drift, and `mypy` uses them to find bugs without running the code

// TODO(gp): Check Gao et al. (2017): that Flow and TypeScript detect about 15%
// of the public bugs in the studied JavaScript projects. It is recalled from
// memory and is not in the notes
- @Fact@: a static type system would have caught about 15% of a sample of fixed
  bugs in JavaScript projects _(Gao et al., 2017)_

- @Characteristics@: what to annotate
  - All the signatures of new library code
  - Local variables only when `mypy` cannot infer their type
  - `-> None` when there is no return value, so that `mypy` checks the function
  - No `Any`, which is the same as no type hint

- @Example@: the repo uses the `typing` style, not the PEP 604 syntax
  ```python
  # Bad.
  def f(data: dict, item: str | None) -> tuple:
  # Good.
  def f(data: Dict[str, str], item: Optional[str]) -> Tuple[str, int]:
  ```

* Common `mypy` Errors and Their Fixes
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.type_hints.how_to_guide.md:96 '## Handling the Annoying `Incompatible types in assignment`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.type_hints.how_to_guide.md:139 '## Handling the Annoying `"None" has no attribute`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.type_hints.how_to_guide.md:193 '## Library Without Types'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Error", "Cause", "Fix"),
  rows: (
    ("Incompatible types in assignment", "One variable holds a string, then a list", "Use two variables, e.g., output and output_as_array"),
    ("\"None\" has no attribute \"fit\"", "An attribute set to None in the constructor", "Annotate Optional[...], assert it is not None, then cast()"),
    ("Function \"numpy...array\" is not valid as a type", "np.array used as a type", "Use np.ndarray"),
    ("No library stub file for module", "A third-party library without types", "Add the library to mypy.ini"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 1.3fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

- @Techniques@: when the error is unclear, wrap an expression in
  `reveal_type()`, and `mypy` prints the type it infers

- @Key idea@: fix the type, not the checker
  - `mypy` gives one type to a variable for its whole scope, so reusing a name
    for a new representation is the most common cause

# Unit Testing

* Tests as Executable Specifications
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1185 '* Testing Philosophy: Tests as Executable Specifications'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:625 '\subsection{Testing Philosophy and Motivation}'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:244 '#### Unit Test Everything'

- @Definition@: a unit test is a **specification that a machine can check**
  - It documents what the code should do
  - It is a safety net for refactoring
  - It forces clear interfaces and less coupling

- @Motivation@: a test is the feedback channel that an agent can act on without
  a human
  - The agent runs the test, reads the failure, and fixes the code in a loop
  - A green suite lets a reviewer trust lines that they did not read one by one

- @Fact@: code that matters needs tests, and code that does not matter should
  not be in the repo
  - So all the code in the repo is tested

- @Remark@: tests as feedback for debugging are covered in detail in Lesson
  11.01

* Unit Test Conventions
// From: .claude/skills/testing.rules.md:3 '# Testing Philosophy'
// From: .claude/skills/testing.rules.md:38 '# Naming Conventions'
// From: .claude/skills/testing.rules.md:72 '## Directory Structure'

- @Characteristics@: four invariants
  - _Test one thing_: one test class per function or class, one case per method
  - _Self-contained_: no shared state and no assumed execution order
  - _Outside-in_: test the public behavior first, since it survives refactors
  - _In sync_: renaming the tested code renames the test

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Object", "Name"),
  rows: (
    ("Function parse_limit_range()", "class Test_parse_limit_range"),
    ("Class Config", "class TestConfig"),
    ("Test methods", "test1, test2, ..., with the intent in the docstring"),
    ("Module hgit.py", "test/test_hgit.py"),
    ("Scratch dir", "test/scratch/<TestClass.test_method>/"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: a rule fixes every name, so an agent and a human produce the same
  test layout with nothing to decide

* Sections of a Test Method
// From: .claude/skills/testing.rules.md:448 '## Use Three Sections in Testing Methods'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:662 '\subsubsection{Basic Test Structure}'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=30%}
- @Definition@: four commented sections
  1. Prepare inputs
  2. Prepare outputs
  3. Run test
  4. Check outputs

- @Pros@:
  - A failure points to one section
  - The expected value is in the same place in every test

- @Remark@: write files in the scratch space, never in `/tmp`
::::
:::: {.column width=70%}

// TODO(gp): Check the test: `_add_file_in_branch()` is a helper invented for
// the illustration
```python
def test1(self) -> None:
    """
    Test a branch that adds one file.
    """
    # Prepare inputs.
    dir_name = self.get_scratch_space()
    _add_file_in_branch(dir_name, "a.py")
    # Prepare outputs.
    expected = "a.py"
    # Run test.
    files = hgit.get_modified_files_in_branch(
        "master", dir_name=dir_name
    )
    # Check outputs.
    actual = "\n".join(files)
    self.assert_equal(actual, expected)
```

::::
:::

* Compare the Whole Output
// From: .claude/skills/testing.rules.md:809 '## Compare Whole Output with `assert_equal`, Not Piecewise'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.in_15_mins.helpers_hunit_test.md:251 '## Text Comparison Modes'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: piecewise checks pass while the rest of the output is wrong
  ```python
  # Bad: misses any line not listed.
  self.assertIn('"A" -> "B"', actual)
  self.assertIn('"B" -> "C"', actual)
  ```

- @Solution@: render the output as text and compare all of it
  ```python
  # Good.
  expected = """
  digraph {
      "A" -> "B";
      "B" -> "C";
  }
  """
  self.assert_equal(
      actual, expected,
      dedent=True, fuzzy_match=True
  )
  ```
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Techniques@: options of `assert_equal()`
  - `fuzzy_match=True`: ignore whitespace
  - `purify_text=True`: replace the Git root, the user name, and memory
    addresses with placeholders
  - `dedent=True`: indent the expected string with the code

- @Key idea@: a whole-output diff shows side effects far from the edit, which
  a piecewise check hides
::::
:::

* Golden File Testing
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1250 '* Golden File Testing and Test Reporting'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:686 '\subsection{Golden File Testing with check\_string}'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.in_15_mins.helpers_hunit_test.md:100 '## How `--update_outcomes` Works'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: a **golden file** stores the full expected output of a test in
  `test/outcomes/<TestClass.test_method>/output/test.txt`
  - Aka "snapshot testing", "approval testing"

- @Pros@: why it suits agents
  - The diff is concrete, so the agent can judge if a change was intended
  - `pytest --update_outcomes` accepts an intended change in one step

- @Remark@: the current repo rules prefer `assert_equal()` with the expected
  string in the test file, and both styles compare the whole output
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph Golden {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.28;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // process : teal | output : blue | pass : green | fail : coral

    Run   [label="check_string(actual)",
           fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Upd   [label="--update_outcomes?", shape=diamond, style="filled",
           fillcolor="#FFFFFF", color="#9AA9B8", fontcolor="#243B53"];
    Write [label="Write test.txt\nand git add",
           fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Cmp   [label="Compare with\ngolden file",
           fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Pass  [label="Pass",
           fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Fail  [label="Fail with diff",
           fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    Run -> Upd;
    Upd -> Write [label="  Yes"];
    Upd -> Cmp [label="  No"];
    Cmp -> Pass [label="  Match"];
    Cmp -> Fail [label="  Mismatch"];
}
```
label=fig:golden-file
caption=A golden file test either updates the stored output (blue) or compares with it and passes (green) or fails (coral)

::::
:::

* Test Categories: Fast, Slow, Superslow
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:794 '\subsection{Test Categorization and Execution}'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.in_15_mins.helpers_hunit_test.md:307 '## Test Speed Tiers'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:837 '\subsubsection{Timeout and Retry Behavior}'

// TODO(gp): Check the timeouts and the cadence: `pytest.ini` and the hunit_test
// blog say ~30 s and ~3600 s, while part1.code_quality.tex says < 20 s and
// < 5 min, and runs the slow tests daily instead of before a merge
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Tier", "Marker", "Timeout", "Typical test", "When it runs"),
  rows: (
    ("Fast", "none", "5 s", "One function", "Each commit and PR"),
    ("Slow", "@pytest.mark.slow", "30 s", "Integration", "Daily"),
    ("Superslow", "@pytest.mark.superslow", "3600 s", "End-to-end", "Weekly"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.8fr, 1.9fr, 0.8fr, 1.1fr, 1.2fr),
  width: 100%,
)
```

- @Techniques@: running a tier, inside the Docker container
  ```bash
  > invoke run_fast_tests --pytest-opts="helpers/test/test_hgit.py"
  ```
  - `pytest-timeout` kills a hung test, and `pytest-rerunfailures` retries a
    failed test (twice for fast tests, once for the others)

- @Key idea@: the tier sets how often a test gives feedback, so an agent in a
  loop runs the fast tier after each edit

- @Remark@: the CI pipeline for the tiers is covered in detail in Lesson 16.01

* Writing Tests With an Agent
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1223 '* Writing and Extending Unit Tests with AI'
// From: .claude/skills/testing.reach_coverage/SKILL.md:34 '## Design a Testing Plan'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:845 '\subsection{Mocking Philosophy and Practice}'

- @Problem@: asked for tests with no constraint, a model often writes tests
  that assert little, mock internal code, and repeat the setup in each method

- @Algorithm@: `testing.write_unit_tests` and `testing.reach_coverage` plan
  first, then implement
  1. Measure the baseline and list the lines that no test runs
  2. Map each uncovered path to a proposed test
  3. Specify the class, method, input, and expected output, with no code
  4. Wait for the approval of a human
  5. Implement, measure again, and report the gaps

- @Techniques@: constraints in the plan
  - Mock only external dependencies (e.g., third-party APIs, S3, databases),
    at the call site, never internal code
  - Do not test assertions and invalid input, unless correctness depends on
    them

// TODO(gp): Check Alshahwan et al. (2024): that TestGen-LLM keeps a test only
// if it builds, passes reliably, and raises coverage. It is recalled from
// memory and is not in the notes
- @Fact@: Meta's **TestGen-LLM** keeps a generated test only if it builds,
  passes reliably, and raises coverage _(Alshahwan et al., 2024)_

# Code Coverage

* Coverage: Definition and Measurement
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/in_30_mins.Python_Code_Coverage.md:38 '## Text report'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/in_30_mins.Python_Code_Coverage.md:284 '## Gotchas'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:912 '\subsection{Test Coverage Measurement and Enforcement}'

- @Definition@: **coverage** is the fraction of the code that the tests run
  - _Statement coverage_: $Cover \triangleq \frac{Stmts - Miss}{Stmts}$
  - _Branch coverage_: each `if` must go both ways (`--cov-branch`)

- @Example@: measure with `pytest-cov`, then report with `coverage`
  ```bash
  > pytest --cov=helpers --cov-branch helpers/test
  > coverage report
  Name           Stmts   Miss  Cover
  my_module.py      10      2    80%
  > coverage html
  ```
  - `coverage html` writes `htmlcov/index.html` with covered, missed, and
    partial lines in color

- @Problem@: a file that no test imports is missing from the report, not shown
  at 0%
  - `--include` only filters the data that was collected
  - `--cov=<dir>` sets the set of files, so untouched files show up at 0%
  - E.g., one dir dropped from 55% to 30% once its untouched files counted

* Coverage Gates in CI
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1136 '* Code Coverage Tracking and Enforcement'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/testing_profiling/all.codecov.how_to_guide.md:98 '## Codecov Configuration'
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:500 '\subsection{Enforced Thresholds and Quality Gates}'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: each test suite uploads its coverage to Codecov under its own
  **flag**
  - `fast` and `slow` run daily, `superslow` runs weekly
  - `carryforward` reuses the last report of a suite that did not run

- @Characteristics@: two gates on each PR
  - _Project_: fails if the total coverage drops by 1% or more against the
    base branch
  - _Patch_: scores the lines that the PR changes

- @Remark@: the gate is a delta, not an absolute target
  - E.g., a legacy codebase at low coverage can still forbid a regression

- @Fact@: the coverage uploads even when tests fail, so there is no blind spot
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```yaml
flag_management:
  individual_flags:
    - name: fast
      carryforward: true
    - name: slow
      carryforward: true
    - name: superslow
      carryforward: true
coverage:
  status:
    project:
      default:
        target: auto
        threshold: 1%
        branches:
          - master
    patch: true
```

::::
:::

* What Coverage Does Not Measure
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part1.code_quality.tex:947 '\subsubsection{Interpreting Coverage Metrics}'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1136 '* Code Coverage Tracking and Enforcement'

- @Problem@: coverage counts the lines that ran, not the lines that were checked
  ```python
  def test1(self) -> None:
      # Runs every line and asserts nothing: 100% covered.
      hgit.get_modified_files_in_branch("master")
  ```

// TODO(gp): Check Inozemtseva et al. (2014): that coverage is only weakly
// correlated with fault detection once the suite size is controlled. It is
// recalled from memory and is not in the notes
- @Fact@: on large Java projects, coverage was only weakly correlated with the
  ability of a suite to detect faults, once the size of the suite was
  controlled _(Inozemtseva et al., 2014)_

- @Characteristics@: the target is behavioral coverage, not 100% line coverage
  - Defensive assertions and impossible states can stay uncovered
  - Critical logic needs exhaustive tests, and a small utility may not

- @Key idea@: an agent told to "raise coverage" can meet the number with tests
  that assert nothing, so the metric needs a second check

* Mutation Testing
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1165 '* Mutation Testing: Verifying That Tests Actually Assert'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Testing_Agents.md:87 '## Research Topics'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Use_agents_for_unit_test_coverage.md:48 '## Implementation plan'

- @Definition@: **mutation testing** plants a small fault, a **mutant**, in the
  code and reruns the tests _(DeMillo et al., 1978; Jia et al., 2011)_
  - E.g., `return n > 0` becomes `return n >= 0`: does any test fail?
  - The suite **kills** the mutant if a test fails
  - A surviving mutant marks logic that no test checks

- @Definition@: the **mutation score** is
  $$MS \triangleq \frac{Killed}{Mutants - Equivalent}$$
  - An _equivalent_ mutant behaves like the original, so no test can kill it

- @Techniques@: `mutmut` and `cosmic-ray` for Python, run only on the lines
  that a PR changes, since a full run is too slow to gate a merge

- @Key idea@: coverage proves that a line ran, and mutation testing proves that
  a test would notice if the line were wrong
  - The mutation score is the acceptance test for tests written by an agent

* Flaky Tests: Detection and Quarantine
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1298 '* Detecting and Quarantining Flaky Tests'

// TODO(gp): Check Luo et al. (2014): that async waits, concurrency, and test
// order dependency are the top causes. It is recalled from memory and is not
// in the notes
- @Definition@: a **flaky test** passes and fails on the same code
  - Common causes: async waits, concurrency, and dependence on the test order
    _(Luo et al., 2014)_

- @Problem@: a flaky test is neither broken nor fine
  - Rerunning it by hand burns the time that the test should save
  - A suite that turns red for no reason trains the team and the agent to
    ignore red

- @Algorithm@: triage before paging a human
  1. Rerun the test alone and many times, to separate order dependence from
     true flakiness
  2. Compare with the history of the same test, e.g., the Allure reports
  3. Classify: real regression, environment flake, or known flake
  4. File a bug for a regression, and quarantine a known flake with a tracking
     issue, never with a silent skip

- @Key idea@: a quarantined test with an open issue is better than a flaky test
  that everyone ignores

# Keeping the Agent Honest

* When the Agent Games the Gate
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:1063 '* Type Checking as a Quality Gate'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:800 '#### When to Disable Linter Messages'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.fix_pyright/SKILL.md:16 '## Fix Lints From `pyright`'

- @Problem@: an agent told to make the checks pass optimizes for green, not for
  correct code

// TODO(gp): Check the shortcuts: they are common failure modes described from
// experience, not measured in a study
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Shortcut", "Example", "Counter"),
  rows: (
    ("Silence a check", "# type: ignore, # pylint: disable", "Require a comment with the error and the reason"),
    ("Weaken a test", "assertIn instead of assert_equal", "Review test diffs like code"),
    ("Accept a wrong output", "--update_outcomes on a bug", "Review the golden file diff"),
    ("Pad coverage", "Tests that assert nothing", "Mutation score next to coverage"),
    ("Skip the gate", "--no-verify, lower the threshold", "CI reruns the checks, deny edits to the gates"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.5fr, 1.7fr),
  width: 100%,
)
```

- @Key idea@: put each gate where the agent cannot edit it, and review the diff
  of the tests as closely as the diff of the code

* Running Example: The Final Version
// From: helpers_root/helpers/hgit.py:1480 'def get_modified_files_in_branch('

```python
def get_modified_files_in_branch(
    dst_branch: str,
    *,
    dir_name: str = ".",
    remove_files_non_present: bool = True,
) -> List[str]:
    """
    Return the files modified with respect to `dst_branch`.

    :param dst_branch: branch to compare to, e.g., `master`
    :param dir_name: dir with the Git client
    :return: modified files
    """
    cmd = f"git diff --name-only {dst_branch}...HEAD"
    return hsystem.system_to_files(
        cmd, dir_name, remove_files_non_present
    )
```

* Which Rung Enforces Each Fix

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Problem in the draft", "Fix", "Rung that enforces it"),
  rows: (
    ("from helpers.hsystem import *", "import helpers.hsystem as hsystem", "amp_check_import, in the hooks and in CI"),
    ("get_git_... in hgit", "get_modified_files_in_branch", "Style guide, coding.rename"),
    ("dir, remove", "dir_name, remove_files_non_present", "Style guide, human review"),
    ("No type hints", "Annotated signature", "amp_mypy"),
    ("Descriptive one-line docstring", "REST docstring in imperative mood", "amp_doc_formatter, coding.fix_docstring"),
    ("No test", "A test with four sections, in the fast tier", "Unit tests and the coverage gate in CI"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 1.6fr, 1.6fr),
  width: 100%,
)
```

- @Key idea@: only one fix needed a human, so the reviewer reads the logic and
  not the form

* Summary

- @Key idea@: an agent multiplies the code to review, so every check of form
  must run without a human
- Conventions are decisions made once: names, spelling, the form of comments and
  docstrings, one import style, and type hints
- The linter turns stable conventions into modifying and non-modifying actions,
  run after each agent edit, before each commit, and again in CI
- Full-path imports, canonical aliases, and cycle detection keep the import
  graph readable and acyclic, and `mypy` checks the types
- Unit tests are executable specifications: fixed names, four sections,
  whole-output comparison, golden files, and speed tiers
- Coverage gates block regressions per suite, mutation testing checks that the
  tests assert, and flaky tests are quarantined, not ignored
- An agent games any gate it can edit, so keep the gates out of its reach and
  review the test diffs like code

